  },
  q85_implement_floyd_warshall_algorithm: {
    id: "q85_implement_floyd_warshall_algorithm",
    num: "85",
    title: "Implement Floyd-Warshall Algorithm",
    tag: "Floyd-Warshall",
    category: "Graphs",
    subtitle: "All-Pairs Shortest Paths on Directed Graph",
    image: "floyd_warshall_graph.png",
    brief: `Implement Floyd-Warshall (all pairs shortest path algorithm).

Given a weighted, directed graph find the shortest distance between every pair of vertices in it, and return the result[ ][ ] matrix where result[u][v] denotes the shortest path from vertex u to vertex v.

Note: if a vertex is unreachable then the shortest distance till that vertex would be considered as -1.

Input Format:
First line contains two space separated integers V, E denoting the number of vertices and edges in the graph.
Following E lines contain three space separated integers u, v, w denoting a directed edge from u to v and its weight w.

Constraints:
1 <= V <= 100
0 <= E <= 1000
0 <= u, v < 100
1 <= w <= 10

Output Format:
Print the result[][] matrix where result[u][v] denotes the shortest path from vertex u to vertex v.

Explanation:
From vertex 0:
• shortest distance to reach vertex 0 is 0
• shortest distance to reach vertex 1 is 7 (0->2->1)
• shortest distance to reach vertex 2 is 3 (0->2)
• shortest distance to reach vertex 3 is 9 (0->2->1->3)

From vertex 1 the shortest distance to all other vertices is: [-1, 0, -1, 2]
From vertex 2 the shortest distance to all other vertices is: [-1, 4, 0, 6]
From vertex 3 the shortest distance to all other vertices is: [-1, -1, -1, 0]`,
